WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.085
Each cloud ships its own private DNS,
with its own console, API, zone model

2
00:00:05.085 --> 00:00:10.375
and failure behavior, and none was
designed to share policy with the others.

3
00:00:10.375 --> 00:00:15.950
The slide compares AWS, Azure,
Google Cloud and VMware NSX.

4
00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.510
Azure private endpoints
are the most common

5
00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.000
source of split-brain,
so they get the detail.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.319
The private answer only comes back from a

7
00:00:23.319 --> 00:00:25.775
resolver that can see
a linked private zone.

8
00:00:25.775 --> 00:00:27.725
Three behaviors catch teams out.

9
00:00:27.725 --> 00:00:32.500
DNS answers don't grant access;
the service's network setting does.

10
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.388
Use one zone per service type everywhere,
with Microsoft's recommended names.

11
00:00:37.388 --> 00:00:42.495
And watch the NXDOMAIN trap: a partner's
storage account can lead into your empty

12
00:00:42.495 --> 00:00:47.590
zone and fail, which is fixed by enabling
fallback to internet on the zone's link.
